18-22 RESERVED for the viscoelastic add-on

23  coordinate system for 2D elements:
    0: Cartesian planar (x,y)
    1: Cylindrical (z,r), axisymmetrical
    Note that for cylindrical/axisymmetrical coordinates we have (z,r),
    i.e. the first coordinate is the axial coordinate z and the second
    coordinate is the radial coordinate r. The third coordinate is theta,
    but is not needed explicitly in an axisymmetrical flow.

24-29 RESERVED for the viscoelastic add-on

30  Make the DEVSS-G matrix contribution symmetric (alpha=coefficients%r(4)):
    0: yes. This is the default and the equation defining G
               nabla u - G^T = 0
       is multiplied by alpha to obtain a symmetric matrix.
    1: no. The coefficients alpha only appears in the contribution to
       the momentum balance. Setting alpha=0 the latter will be zero, whereas
       G will still be defined. This is needed if the equations are solved
       fully implicit without DEVSS-G, but G is still needed for the
       constitutive equation (G-method).
    NOTE: this only applies to DEVSS-G, not to DEVSS-E.

31  RESERVED for the viscoelastic add-on

32  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(10) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate 
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by 
        coefficients%i(10) to each subdomain.

33  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    boundary integrals:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(11) to the complete element domain. 
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate 
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by 
        coefficients%i(11) to each subdomain.

34  Number of degrees of freedom in the element in one direction (ndf) of the
    velocity interpolation is different from the number of nodes in an element.
    0: no
    1: yes. This is tricky stuff, but it might work. The problem is
       isoparametric mapping of the elements, where it is assumed that the
       shape functions of the mapping are identical to the velocity shape
       functions. At the moment it is assumed that the first 1:ndf nodes are
       the nodes of the velocity unknowns, in that order. In this way it is
       possible to use elements with an additional internal node without a
       velocity unknown, provided that it has the highest nodal number.
       A more general mechanism for mixing different shape functions/element
       types is planned but is not available yet.

35  interpolation for the geometrical shape of elements in an object

36  the direction of the outside normal vector (for example for the open
    boundary condition) or the normal vector directing outside of the
    contributing part of an interface:
     0: default: the same as giving 1
    -1: opposite to the direction given by the curve or surface
     1: as given by the curve or surface

37  Numerical integration rule and number of subdomains for the
    volume integrals of elements:
     0: the same for all elements as given by coefficients%i(10) and
        coefficients%i(32)
     1: given by the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)
     2: given by user subroutines

38  Layer number. Needs to be specified if the elements are going to be used
    (build,derive etc) for a single layer only.
     0: default: all degrees
     >0: layer number. 

39  Build the continuity equation (div u=0):
    0: Yes (default)
    1: No 
    NOTES: 
     - this only affects the continuity equation; the pressure term (grad p)
       is always build.
     - the system becomes singular and other elements need to be used to fill
       in the zero lines of the matrix, for example using separate elements
       for compressible flow.

40  Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle, hexahedron->pyramid).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)
    NOTE: this affects all numerical integration of interior and boundary
    integrals.

41  numerical integration rule for embedded boundary integrals using an eltree
    Note: in 2D interfaces consist of line intervals and in 3D of triangles.

42  The vector (coefficient) ubar (=prescribed velocity in the embedded
    Diriclet boundary condition) is as follows:
    <0 ubar = 0 (i.e. rhs vector = 0, which saves CPU time)
    0: constant, given by coefficients%r(12:14)
    1: given by a vector function, vfuncnr=coefficient%i(43)
       NOTE: vector function must be assigned to coefficients%vfunc1(1)%p
    2: given by nodal values in the vector oldvectors%v(1)
    3: given by Gauss values in the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)

43  vfuncnr for ubar (=prescribed velocity for embedded Dirichlet bc)

44  sign of the "transposed" open boundary viscous term
    (elements stokes_open_boundary_transposed_eltree and
     stokes_traction_interface_transposed_eltree):
    0: default (same as 1)
    1: symmetric version (like in the Nitsche method)
   -1: anti-symmetric version (like in the Baumann-Oden method)
    
45  sign of the "transposed" open boundary pressure term
    (elements stokes_open_boundary_transposed_eltree and
     stokes_traction_interface_transposed_eltree):
    0: default (term is absent, i.e. set to zero).
    1: symmetric version
   -1: anti-symmetric version
    
46  add the symmetric "transposed" open boundary viscous term
    (element stokes_open_boundary_eltree_particle):
    0: default (term is absent, i.e. set to zero).
    1: use symmetric version (like in the Nitsche method)
   -1: use anti-symmetric version (like in the Baumann-Oden method)

47  Specifies which layer is contributing to the interface condition for this
    call to the system build (clayer):
    0: default: the same as specifying 1
    1: the first layer
    2: the second layer
    NOTE: this is used in stokes_traction_interface_eltree and
          stokes_traction_interface_transposed_eltree
          to avoid specifying material properties/functions/coefficients etc.
          on both sides in one routine.
    NOTE: this requires only two layers to be present at the interface, or at
          least specified in the build (not available yet in build_system).

48  Use mesh/grid velocity for ALE:
    0 No
    1 Yes. The mesh/grid velocity is given by the vectors oldvectors%v(1) and
      oldvectors%v(2), where the latter is for the previous time step
      (if needed for higher order time stepping).
    2 Yes. The mesh/grid velocity is derived from the temporary ALE meshes:
         mesh_ALE_np1 = oldvectors%m(1) 
         mesh_ALE_n   = oldvectors%m(3)
      where the latter is for the previous time step (if needed for higher order
      time stepping). 
      The computational meshes are given by
         mesh_n   = oldvectors%m(2)
         mesh_nm1 = oldvectors%m(4) 
      where the latter is for the previous time step (if needed for higher order
      time stepping). The current computational mesh is the mesh supplied
      through build. Note, that mesh_n and mesh_nm1 needs to be supplied even
      if the computational mesh does not change.
      NOTE: this coefficient is not for the stokes add-on, but for add-ons that
      depend on it. It might also not work for all add-ons yet.

